WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:04.727
Teacher midterm practice for C plus plus, question 25.

2
00:00:05.347 --> 00:00:10.018
On the course compiler, Code Blocks with MinGW on Windows, what are the

3
00:00:10.018 --> 00:00:14.926
smallest value, the size, and the largest value of an unsigned long?

4
00:00:16.626 --> 00:00:20.488
Unsigned means no negative values, so the smallest is zero.

5
00:00:21.109 --> 00:00:24.998
On Windows compilers, long is four bytes, which is thirty two bits.

6
00:00:24.998 --> 00:00:26.611
You can check with size of.

7
00:00:27.231 --> 00:00:31.203
Thirty two bits hold two to the thirty two patterns, from zero to two

8
00:00:31.203 --> 00:00:35.067
to the thirty two minus one: about four point three billion.

9
00:00:36.667 --> 00:00:42.258
The theory: an n bit unsigned type ranges from zero to two to the n, minus one.

10
00:00:42.258 --> 00:00:45.529
A signed type gives half its range to negatives.

11
00:00:45.529 --> 00:00:48.265
And sizes depend on the platform: on sixty

12
00:00:48.265 --> 00:00:51.477
four bit Linux or Mac, long is eight bytes.

13
00:00:53.077 --> 00:00:57.652
So the answer is zero, four bytes, and four billion, two hundred ninety four

14
00:00:57.652 --> 00:01:01.968
million, nine hundred sixty seven thousand, two hundred ninety five.

15
00:01:02.788 --> 00:01:06.737
Assuming long is eight bytes everywhere. Always check with size of.

16
00:01:07.457 --> 00:01:09.693
Exam tip: remember the powers.

17
00:01:09.693 --> 00:01:13.237
Two to the thirty one is about two point one billion, and two

18
00:01:13.237 --> 00:01:16.454
to the thirty two is about four point three billion.
